nd street.\n\n**3. Southwest near Bldg. 8 (West of Unit 801)**\n*   **Drainage Direction:** The slope continues the east-to-west downhill trend. Bldg. 8 has an FFE of 680.50. Spot elevations near the pad are 679.98 and 679.46, grading down to 679.75 and 679.25 near the sidewalk.\n*   **TOWARD or AWAY FROM:** Drainage flows **TOWARD** the pad from the east (Bldg. 8) and **AWAY FROM** the pad to the west/southwest.\n*   **Potential Liquid Flow Issues:** Liquid from this pad would flow westward toward the sidewalk and the street. Because it is near the lowest western edge of the site (approaching the 678 contour), pooling could occur near the curb/retaining wall transition at the southwest corner.\n\n**4. East central between Bldg. 4 and 5 (East of Unit 405, West of Unit 504)**\n*   **Drainage Direction:** The ground slopes steeply from west to east in this interior section. Bldg. 4 (to the west) has an FFE of 685.50. The spot elevations near the pad drop to 683.75 and 683.25. Directly east of the pad is Bldg. 5 (Unit 504), which sits at a lower FFE of 683.00. \n*   **TOWARD or AWAY FROM:** Drainage flows **TOWARD** the pad from Bldg. 4 (west) and continues to flow **AWAY FROM** the pad but directly **TOWARD** Bldg. 5 (east).\n*   **Potential Liquid Flow Issues:** This presents a significant liquid flow issue. Because the pad sits at a higher elevation (~683.25 - 683.75) than the adjacent downstream structure (Bldg. 5 at 683.00), any oil leak would flow eastward and pool directly against the foundation/facade of Unit 504.\n\n**5. Southeast near Bldg. 7 (East of Unit 705)**\n*   **Drainage Direction:** The site slopes from west to east toward the property boundary. Bldg. 7 has an FFE of 685.00. Spot elevations around the pad are 682.75 and 682.25. Just east of this pad is an \"Existing Retaining Wall to be Replaced\" with a Top of Wall (TW) elevation of 680.26 and an Existing Grade (EG) of 669.84 below it.\n*   **TOWARD or AWAY FROM:** Drainage flows **TOWARD** the pad 