ell.\n\n**3) Are there any vertical clearance dimensions shown from the finished grade or road surface to the overhead electric line?**\n**No.** A thorough scan of the entire length of the utility line along Valley View Road reveals no vertical clearance dimensions. While there are numerous surface elevation callouts in the vicinity—such as Top of Curb (**\"TC: 682.74\"**), Top of Wall (**\"TW 683.77\"**), contour lines (e.g., **\"684\"**, **\"683\"**), and Finished Grade elevations (e.g., **\"FG 680.13\"**)—there are no dimension lines, notes, or callouts indicating the height of the overhead wires above these surfaces.\n\n**4) Are there any section views or profile details that show vertical clearances under the overhead line?**\n**No.** The provided sheet (Sheet 11 of 39, titled **\"Grading Plan\"**) consists entirely of a single, top-down plan view. There are no section cuts, profile views, inset boxes, or detail drawings included on this specific page that would illustrate vertical clearances."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d43314f-ffee-4c09-9531-73169237fb4a","uuid":"3adaabab-beb9-484d-aafc-c74279723476","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a meticulous visual analysis of the provided \"Grading Plan\" document, here are the answers to your specific questions:\n\n**1) The location of the overhead electric line (OHE) shown on the grading plan:**\nBased on a strict textual review of the main drawing, legend, and labels, there is no explicit text label reading \"OHE\", \"Overhead Electric\", or a specific line type identified as such in the legend on this particular sheet. \n\nHowever, looking at standard engineering symbology present on the plan, there is a line connecting circular symbols containing a dot (standard representation for utility poles) that runs north-south along the far western edge of the site. This line is located outside the western property boundary, running roughly parallel to the street labeled **\"Valley View Ro