{"level":30,"time":1776705576804,"pid":50886,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01WmPeTiDCiHGuyT9hpzptUL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, so on the WEST side there's a water line (W), not an overhead electric line. But the electrical plan (Sheet 21) indicated there should be an OHE line on the west boundary. Let me check if the OHE line is visible or maybe just outside the site boundary (on the street right-of-way).\n\nThe key findings so far:\n1. **South boundary:** Mitigation trees are 0-0.2 feet from OHE line - MAJOR VIOLATION\n2. **West boundary:** SY trees are 10-15 feet from the water line (not overhead electric)\n\nThe west side doesn't seem to have overhead electric lines shown on the landscape plan itself - the water line is the primary utility shown. The overhead electric on the west may be beyond the property line on the street.\n\nNow, I still need to determine:\n1. What species are the three mitigation trees on the south boundary?\n2. Are they utility-compatible?\n3. What are their mature heights?\n\nThe plant schedule on Sheet 35 shows:\n- Cedar Elm and Live Oak (shade trees - these are large trees 40+ ft)\n- Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud (ornamental trees - these are smaller 20-30 ft)\n- Plus existing trees being preserved\n\nThe mitigation trees being planted are likely the shade trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) which are large (40+ ft mature height).\n\nIf the three mitigation trees on the south boundary are:\n- Live Oak or Cedar Elm (which are both large trees ≥40 ft mature height)\n- They are 0-0.2 feet from the OHE line\n\nThen this FAILS EL-2.3: \"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment\" - because they are within the 25-foot zone and definitely within the 0.2 feet measured.\n\nLet me try to get more information about which specific species are the mitigation trees on the south boundary.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1763,"cache_read_input_tokens":69869,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1763},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b09093e-77f3-4183-964d-6ca1d8b22912","uuid":"f107d3d8-edf1-4004-bea1-ecaa04c234ca"}
