{"level":30,"time":1776705799583,"pid":50886,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01JJSCvYPbj1ARAJcNkwnWbA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Okay, so:\n1. The 12 @ 4\" trees are the 2 Live Oaks + 10 Cedar Elms (shade trees)\n2. The 8 @ 2.5\" trees are the 2 Texas Mountain Laurels + 6 Mexican Redbuds (ornamental trees)\n3. Sheet 35 doesn't specify where these trees are planted or their mature heights\n4. Sheet 35 doesn't reference ECM Appendix F utility compatibility for the proposed species\n\nNow, the critical question is: which of these tree species are planted in the three mitigation trees on the south boundary that are 0-0.2 feet from the OHE line?\n\nFrom the Sheet 31 landscape plan vision analysis, it was clear that there are \"M\" (Mitigation) tagged trees on the south boundary. The plant list shows 20 total new trees (12 shade + 8 ornamental), and 55 inches of mitigation are provided on site.\n\nNow, I know from horticultural standards that:\n- **Live Oak (Quercus virginiana)** - Mature height 40-60 feet - LARGE TREE\n- **Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia)** - Mature height 40-60 feet - LARGE TREE\n- **Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora)** - Mature height 20-25 feet - NOT LARGE\n- **Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana)** - Mature height 20-30 feet - NOT LARGE\n\nSo if the three mitigation trees on the south boundary are either Live Oak or Cedar Elm, they are LARGE trees (≥40 ft) and therefore violate EL-2.3.\n\nEven if they are Texas Mountain Laurel or Mexican Redbud (which are not large), they still violate EL-2.1 if they are non-utility-compatible trees, because they are within 15 feet of the overhead electric distribution conductor.\n\n\nThe 0-0.2 foot setback itself constitutes a violation regardless of species. Without knowing the exact species placement at the south boundary, I can't pinpoint which specific code section applies, but the proximity violation is definitive. For EL-2.2, transmission lines would need to be 60kV or higher—the site shows distribution-level utilities (12.47kV, 34.5kV), so this likely doesn't apply.\n\nEL-2.5 requires checking for transmission easements; the plat only shows 5-foot electric easements. EL-2.6 involves utility poles along the overhead line on the south boundary—I need to verify if any trees fall within 10 feet of these. EL-2.7 requires examining the transformer pads shown on Sheet 21 to see if proposed trees are within 10 feet of them. EL-2.8 addresses underground utility clearances, which I should evaluate next.\n\nFor EL-2.9, trees within 20 feet of underground equipment need 4-foot root barriers—I'll need to check the landscape details for these specifications. The plant list on Sheet 35 appears to address EL-2.10 by identifying all species with a defined legend, which seems compliant. However, EL-2.11 may be problematic since the list shows minimum heights but doesn't explicitly identify which trees are \"large\" (40+ feet at maturity). EL-2.12 involves clear sight distance requirements that I should examine next. Looking at utility pole and pad-mounted equipment placements to identify additional compliance gaps.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1804,"cache_read_input_tokens":71632,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1804},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b09093e-77f3-4183-964d-6ca1d8b22912","uuid":"ad1c906d-08e5-422e-9519-42b4850233dd"}
